<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hummay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1075–<a href="/years/1086">1086</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Humai, Hume</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hummay was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> in the late eleventh century and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a>, which ruled Kanem and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> until the nineteenth century. Later tradition sometimes calls him the first Muslim ruler of the kingdom, but his predecessors were already Muslims, as the mahram, a charter of privileges granted to the family of the missionary Muhammad ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, also shows. His accession ended the rule of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duguwa-dynasty">Duguwa dynasty</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most versions of the girgam, the royal chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>, make Hummay the son of his direct predecessor, Selema I, also called Abd al-Jalil, and several sources call him ibn Abd al-Jalil or give him the epithet Jilmi, son of Jil. His mother, Tigiram, came from the Kay or Koyam people of Dirkou. The chronicle closes its account of Selema with the words that it will now set down the history of the Banū Ḥummay, and the change of dynasty has therefore often been read, as Barth read it in 1857, as a change of religion within one royal lineage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>What the dynastic shift means remains unclear. Hummay may have overthrown Selema and been linked to the old line only later, as his supposed son, in the royal chronicle. The historian Hiribarren suggested in 2016 that he took power with the help of a pro-Islamic faction at court. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> is generally thought to have been founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaghawa">Zaghawa</a>, who appear in the 12th century as separate nomads in the north-east, his rise has also been linked to a possible expulsion of the Zaghawa, with Selema as their last ruler of Kanem.</p><p>Although Hummay was of local origin, possibly Kanembu, he and his dynasty claimed descent from the Yemeni noble Saif ibn Dhi Yazan, a claim to Arab origin of the kind other African Muslim dynasties of his time also made for prestige. The further spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in his reign may have caused dissension, leading the Toubou to break from imperial rule and move east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hummay is recorded to have died in Masr, that is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and one source names the place as Rukana, of many mosques, which suggests that he meant to make, or had made, the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. He was succeeded by his son Dunama I Umemi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> that later tradition traced to him ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem–Bornu Empire</a> until 1846, one of the longest-ruling dynasties in world history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hummay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling house adopted Islam, traditionally under <b>Mai Hummay</b> around 1085, which strengthened diplomatic and commercial ties with Muslim states across the Sahara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
